// Grey Duster: a plain British hackled dry: blue-grey rabbit body and a badger hackle.

id grey-duster
name "Grey Duster"
aliases "Grey Dusty" "Duster"
type dry
imitates mayfly attractor
difficulty beginner
species trout grayling
season may june july august september
origin region="Wales or England"
description "A carrot-shaped body of blue-grey rabbit underfur and a badger cock hackle, without tail or wings."
history """
    An old British pattern of unknown origin. Scott Sadil traces it to anglers on the upper
    Dee in Wales, a century or more ago; Fish4Flies gives England and says it began as a
    stillwater midge. Neither gives a source. Courtney Williams ranked it with the best
    mayfly imitations or above them, and Stan Headley writes of anglers who use no other fly
    whenever mayflies hatch (both as reported by Sadil).
    """
fishing """
    For rivers and stillwaters. Without a tail the rear end breaks the surface and the fly
    hangs in the film; Bob Wyatt reads it as an emerger rather than a dun. A general mayfly
    imitation, tied small for Caenis, the angler's curse; Preben Torp Jacobsen found a
    parachute version took Caenis feeders that refused everything else. It is hard to see
    once it settles: watch for any rise near it. Andrew Overend fishes it as a searching dry
    for trout and grayling, above all on the Welsh Dee.
    """
sources {
    "California Fly Fisher: At the Vise, Grey Duster (Scott Sadil)" url="https://calflyfisher.com/tips-and-techniques/at-the-vise-grey-duster/"
    "Fish4Flies: Grey Duster" url="https://www.fish4flies.com/Dry/Hackled/Grey-Duster"
    "GlobalFlyFisher: Grey Duster, parachute (Preben Torp Jacobsen)" url="https://globalflyfisher.com/patterns/grey-duster"
    "Hawker-Overend: Grey Duster fly, how to tie 2 productive patterns (Andrew Overend)" url="https://hawker-overend.com/how-to-tie-grey-duster-fly-patterns/"
}
// Photos for checking proportions while authoring (pnpm refcheck); never published.
references {
    "https://www.turrall.com/cdn/shop/files/DH14-14_016d3049-d253-4017-8faa-5aca39db5e25.jpg?v=1777536396" page="https://www.turrall.com/products/grey-duster" license="All rights reserved; reference only" note="Side view; has a tail, which is not traditional"
    "https://www.fish4flies.com/img/flies/Large/875-875.jpg" page="https://www.fish4flies.com/Dry/Hackled/Grey-Duster" license="All rights reserved; reference only" flip=#true note="Side view"
}

// Assumption: a down-eye dry hook; the traditional British choice is an up-eye Captain Hamilton.
hook dry-standard size=14
thread brown size="8/0"

materials {
    body rabbit-dubbing color=blue-dun label="Blue-grey rabbit underfur"
    // Assumption: fibres about 1.6× the gape; no source gives a length.
    hackle cock-hackle color=badger fibre=1.6 label="Badger cock hackle"
}

steps {
    attach-thread at=0.9
    wrap-thread to=0.0
    dub body to=0.78 note="Carrot-shaped: thin at the rear, fatter forward, a little further forward than on a winged fly."
    tie-in hackle note="Webby base stripped, underside facing you."
    wrap-thread to=0.92
    wind hackle to=0.9 turns=5
    tie-off hackle
    whip-finish note="A small, neat head."
}
